{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 3090,
    "dDurationMs": 2470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Astronomers are the worst at naming things."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5560,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mean seriously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7260,
    "dDurationMs": 2110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark energy AND dark matter?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9370,
    "dDurationMs": 1450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Who can remember which is which."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10820,
    "dDurationMs": 4949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But perhaps one astronomer has just fixed\nit, with a theory that says perhaps actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15769,
    "dDurationMs": 1931,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they are they same stuff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22680,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dr. Jamie Farnes, astrophysicist at Oxford just\npublished a paper suggesting that both dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28230,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy and dark matter may result from the\nsame phenomenon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32250,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it’s pretty wild: negative mass particles\ncontinuously popping into existence between"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37760,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39280,
    "dDurationMs": 4939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This rather extravagant claim resulted in\na hysterical response from the media."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44219,
    "dDurationMs": 5461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our viewers’ perfect blend of bright-eyed\ncuriosity and cynical skepticism led to many"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49680,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "MANY requests for us to do an episode on this\nnew result."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53970,
    "dDurationMs": 1089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You got it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55059,
    "dDurationMs": 3371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today on Space Time Journal Club, let’s\npick apart J.S."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58430,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Farnes 2018, “A unifying theory of dark\nenergy and dark matter: Negative masses and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63970,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter creation within a modified Lambda-CDM\nframework”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68320,
    "dDurationMs": 4479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As with any new theory combining dark matter\nand dark energy, probably it helps to know"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72799,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what they are first."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74299,
    "dDurationMs": 4581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This will be a true too-long-didn’t-watch,\nbecause we’ve been over both a bunch."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78880,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, dark matter: the galaxies are spinning\ntoo fast."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83630,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Based on the gravity from visible matter alone\nthey shouldn’t be able to hold themselves"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87710,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88710,
    "dDurationMs": 2479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They should scatter their stars into the void."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91189,
    "dDurationMs": 6350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So we conclude that galaxies, and for that\nmatter the universe, has 5-10 times as much"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97539,
    "dDurationMs": 2201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter as we can actually see."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99740,
    "dDurationMs": 5519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call it dark matter, and try as we might\nwe can’t find the presumably-exotic particle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105259,
    "dDurationMs": 2081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that constitutes it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107340,
    "dDurationMs": 5319,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark energy is completely different – or\nso the standard story goes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112659,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Observations of distant supernovae tell us\nthat the expansion of the universe is accelerating."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 117229,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That acceleration matches the effect you would\nget if empty space itself had a tiny bit of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122799,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 123799,
    "dDurationMs": 4191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As more space comes into existence because\nof that expansion thing the more“dark energy”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127990,
    "dDurationMs": 3139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you get, which causes more acceleration."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131129,
    "dDurationMs": 4911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, we gotta think a bit harder about\nwhy dark energy causes accelerated expansion,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136040,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s important for the new paper."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138880,
    "dDurationMs": 4359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The standard picture of dark energy is that\nit’s an actual greater-than-zero energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143239,
    "dDurationMs": 1890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the vacuum of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145129,
    "dDurationMs": 4521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to Einstein’s general theory of\nrelativity, any such positive energy produces"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149650,
    "dDurationMs": 1809,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an inward-pulling gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151459,
    "dDurationMs": 2420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s true of dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 153879,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why does it result in an outward-pushing,\naccelerating expansion?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158109,
    "dDurationMs": 5091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s a super-subtle point that took us\nlike 5 episodes to explain."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 163200,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The big reveal was in this one, where we pick\napart the second Friedmann equation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167980,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s the solution to the Einstein equations\nthat tells you the rate of acceleration of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172500,
    "dDurationMs": 1330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the expansion of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173830,
    "dDurationMs": 5860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But here’s the punchline: if empty space\nhas a constant, positive energy density then"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179690,
    "dDurationMs": 3139,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it also has a negative pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182829,
    "dDurationMs": 6990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For obscure reasons that we delve into those\nepisodes, negative pressure produces an anti-gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189819,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 190819,
    "dDurationMs": 5741,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So dark energy has competing effects – its\npositive energy density gives it a positive gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196560,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "effect but its negative pressure is anti-gravitational."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200870,
    "dDurationMs": 6410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of dark energy the latter wins,\nand so the expansion of the universe accelerates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207280,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An important part of this is that the energy\ndensity of dark energy is constant, so it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212090,
    "dDurationMs": 2810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doesn’t dilute as the universe expands."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214900,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Einstein’s equations and the Friedmann\nequations, that constant energy density is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219400,
    "dDurationMs": 3559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "represented by the cosmological constant,\nor Lambda."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222959,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And a positive Lambda means accelerating expansion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226939,
    "dDurationMs": 1821,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, enough with the review."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228760,
    "dDurationMs": 1830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s look at the paper."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230590,
    "dDurationMs": 5019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Jamie Farnes was looking for a way to get\nan anti-gravitational effect that explained"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235609,
    "dDurationMs": 2530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both dark energy AND dark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238139,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He tried to do this in very different way:\nwith negative mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242489,
    "dDurationMs": 4091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In physics, we usually assume that mass is\nalways positive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246580,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Forgetting that pressure stuff for a moment,\npositive masses and energies always have a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251560,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "positive gravitational effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253480,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They attract other things."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255680,
    "dDurationMs": 2470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what about negative masses?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258150,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer is complicated - it’s not always\nobvious how weird stuff like negative masses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262900,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "translates from general relativity to Newton’s\nlaws."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266940,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for now let’s follow the paper’s argument,\nwhich is based on Newtonian physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271490,
    "dDurationMs": 5690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In Newtonian gravity, you multiply the two\nmasses together along with some other stuff"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277180,
    "dDurationMs": 3209,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to get the strength of their mutual gravitational\nattraction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280389,
    "dDurationMs": 4671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Two positive masses always give an attractive\nforce by definition."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285060,
    "dDurationMs": 6329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Two negative masses should cancel each other’s\nsigns so also give you an attractive force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291389,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But with one negative and one positive mass,\nthe final force of gravity has the opposite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295889,
    "dDurationMs": 3011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sign - that makes it repulsive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298900,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dr. Farnes takes the Newtonian argument a step\nfurther."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303780,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mass determines the strength and direction\nof the gravitational field – that’s gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308470,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309470,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It also determines the way objects respond\nto forces via Newton’s 2nd Law."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314760,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this equation, mass is inertial mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318160,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It should be the same as gravitational mass\nfor the equivalence principle to hold."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323230,
    "dDurationMs": 6330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a positive inertial mass, direction of\nacceleration is the same as direction as the applied force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329560,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a negative mass, this equation suggests\nthat acceleration is in the opposite direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 334810,
    "dDurationMs": 2020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the applied force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 336830,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Push a negative mass away from you it’ll\nmove towards you, pull it and it’ll move"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342270,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "away."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343270,
    "dDurationMs": 2489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "According to this Newtonian interpretation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345759,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So Farnes argues that the “attractive”\ngravitational force between two negative masses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350599,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should actually drive them apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353039,
    "dDurationMs": 3461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is supposed to give the outward push\nto replace dark energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356500,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the same time, the repulsive force between\na negative and positive mass should repel"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361810,
    "dDurationMs": 3289,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the positive and attract the negative mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365099,
    "dDurationMs": 5022,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s a bizarre situation, but as I’ll\nexplain in a moment it’ll give us our dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370121,
    "dDurationMs": 1138,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "matter replacement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371259,
    "dDurationMs": 4581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact let’s start with dark matter because\nthat’s a bit more straightforward."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375840,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The author uses these ideas about the interactions\nof negative and positive mass particles to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380340,
    "dDurationMs": 2630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "create an N-body simulation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382970,
    "dDurationMs": 5099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Basically, he programs a virtual universe\ninto his computer with both positive and negative"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388069,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass particles, along with his interpretations\nof Newton’s laws."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 392419,
    "dDurationMs": 4631,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those simulations showed that galaxies do\nindeed spin more quickly when surrounded by"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397050,
    "dDurationMs": 2089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negative mass particles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399139,
    "dDurationMs": 5941,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is because the positive mass in the galaxy\nattracts a halo of negative mass, but at the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 405080,
    "dDurationMs": 5559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same time that positive mass is repelled inwards\nby the surrounding halo."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410639,
    "dDurationMs": 4831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This confines the galaxy from the outside\nso it can spin faster than if it were held"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415470,
    "dDurationMs": 2479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "together by its gravity alone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417949,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a pretty cool result."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 419789,
    "dDurationMs": 6291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To make the same negative mass stuff also\nemulate dark energy requires an extra gigantic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426080,
    "dDurationMs": 1500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assumption."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427580,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The problem is that our negative masses will\ndilute away as the universe expands."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432840,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To fix this, Farnes proposes that these negative\nmasses are constantly created as the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439240,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expands."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440240,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That keeps the density of negative masses\nconstant, even as the density of positive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444300,
    "dDurationMs": 1790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "masses falls."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446090,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The result is a very diffuse negative mass\nfluid that fills the universe and constantly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451330,
    "dDurationMs": 1480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "replenishes itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 452810,
    "dDurationMs": 4329,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s no real justification for this,\nbesides a shoutout to Fred Hoyle and Hermann"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457139,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bondi who proposed similar things in their\nseverely debunked steady state universe idea."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463979,
    "dDurationMs": 5711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that’s cool – the physical justification\nbehind dark energy is pretty tenuous too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469690,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, you remember I said that a constant POSITIVE\nenergy density can be expressed as a cosmological"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476050,
    "dDurationMs": 1299,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constant?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477349,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that a positive Lambda results in an antigravitational\nnegative pressure?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482539,
    "dDurationMs": 5671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well a constant NEGATIVE energy density – like\nthe one proposed by Farnes, gives a negative"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488210,
    "dDurationMs": 1820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cosmological constant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490030,
    "dDurationMs": 4949,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That gives a positive pressure, and in general\nrelativity positive pressure adds an attractive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494979,
    "dDurationMs": 3410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravitational force, no matter what causes\nit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498389,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we look at the second Friedmann equation,\nwhich is really the analog of Newton’s law"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502669,
    "dDurationMs": 5171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of gravity for the whole cosmos, this negative\nmass fluid – as a negative cosmological"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507840,
    "dDurationMs": 6290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constant – has the same competing effects\nas regular dark energy, but in the opposite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514130,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515510,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The direct effect is repulsive – antigravitational\n– which I guess was the original motivation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521010,
    "dDurationMs": 1610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for using negative matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522620,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that effect is overwhelmed by the effect\nof the pressure, which in this case is attractive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528090,
    "dDurationMs": 2870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– it works to recollapse the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530960,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now Farnes acknowledges that his proposal\ngives a negative cosmological constant that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ultimately decelerates, but there’s some\ncontradiction because he also suggests that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541920,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it’s a good dark energy replacement because\nit acts like a cosmological constant."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546440,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yet a negative cosmological constant gives\nyou an extremely different universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550320,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dr. Farnes does a couple of calculations that\nare consistent with a negative lambda."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555740,
    "dDurationMs": 4451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He uses the first Friedmann equation – that’s\nthis thing - to correctly conclude that a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560191,
    "dDurationMs": 5769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe with a negative cosmological constant\nshould have a sinusoidal scale factor."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565960,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He interprets that to mean the universe should\noscillate endlessly in size."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570120,
    "dDurationMs": 1590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that’s a bit mixed up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571710,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact that sinusoidal solution is only valid\nfor the bit of the sine wave where the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576640,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is expanding from zero time – the big bang\n- slowing down towards the first peak and then collapsing again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583100,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The negative cosmological constant is the\nsource of that slow-down and for the subsequent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588060,
    "dDurationMs": 1330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accelerating collapse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589390,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s no real oscillation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591750,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The end of this universe is here, where it\ngets back to zero size."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595720,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Plugging negative masses into general relativity\nallows you to break causality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600360,
    "dDurationMs": 6980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can build traversable wormholes, Alcubierre\nwarp fields, time-machines, anti-unicorns."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607350,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a strong indication that negative\nmass can’t exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611400,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so these are my complaints on theoretical\ngrounds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614840,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what about the data?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616620,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Does a universe with a constant negative energy\ndensity fit the observations?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 621200,
    "dDurationMs": 2670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are two main things to check."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623870,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, does it predict an expansion history\nthat fits the supernova observations that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629220,
    "dDurationMs": 2340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "originally discovered dark energy?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631560,
    "dDurationMs": 5330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those supernova results suggest a universe\nthat started expanding rapidly and then slowed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636890,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down due to the gravity of matter – mostly\ndark matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640370,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But then that deceleration turned around as\ndark energy kicked in, resulting in the current"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646530,
    "dDurationMs": 1910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accelerating expansion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648440,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With a negative cosmological constant and\nthis sinusoidal expansion, any slowdown happens"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653800,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "near the turnaround point presumably tens of billions of years in the future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657780,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s hard to fit to the supernova data."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660950,
    "dDurationMs": 6690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Farnes calculates an age for his universe\nof 13.8 billion years assuming a very low"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667640,
    "dDurationMs": 6830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negative energy density, but that really just\ncorresponds to the very first straight part in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674470,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rise in the sine curve, which is almost a\nconstant expansion rate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679540,
    "dDurationMs": 4490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We already know that a constant expansion\nrate happens to give a similar age to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684030,
    "dDurationMs": 1900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "standard dark energy age."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685930,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But a constant or near-constant expansion\nrate definitely does not fit the supernova"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691730,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "data."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692730,
    "dDurationMs": 2460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second thing to check is the cosmic microwave\nbackground."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695190,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m not going to go into this in detail\n– again, we’ve done it before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699260,
    "dDurationMs": 4681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just quickly, the density fluctuations seen\nin the afterglow of the Big Bang reveal a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703941,
    "dDurationMs": 3129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe that is spatially flat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707070,
    "dDurationMs": 4710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dark energy, with its positive energy density,\nwhen added to the energy of both regular and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711780,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dark matter, are needed to explain this spatially\nflat universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717060,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you replace both dark energy and dark matter\nwith negative-energy stuff, then the universe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 722650,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "becomes negatively curved."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725130,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What we call anti-deSitter space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727950,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Which is good for string theorists, as Farnes\nnotes, but not for observers because that’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732640,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not consistent with what we see."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735120,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So I guess I’m saying that the paper had\nsome really interesting ideas, but ultimately"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739660,
    "dDurationMs": 1350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doesn’t hold together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741010,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I think what really bugged me wasn’t the\npaper, but rather the media response."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746280,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It felt like there was no effort at all to\ncheck with other sources about whether this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750700,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was news-worthy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752380,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Super lazy journalism."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754440,
    "dDurationMs": 2860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except for WIRED – they really nailed it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 757300,
    "dDurationMs": 1610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And what about Dr. Jamie Farnes?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758910,
    "dDurationMs": 3030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He’s a talented astrophysicist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761940,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those galaxy rotation simulations are fascinating."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764160,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I think it’s courageous to look at fringe\nideas like this, but maybe don’t do press"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769210,
    "dDurationMs": 3190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "releases without getting triple-checked by\ncosmologists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772400,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m also happy to be corrected on anything\nI said, and I’ll address that in the comment"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777470,
    "dDurationMs": 2170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "responses next time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779640,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After all, it’s easy to get confused by\nthe compounding negatives in what is probably"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783900,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not a negative mass, anti-gravitational, positive\npressure, anti-deSitter space time."
    } ]
  } ]
}
